The '''Self-Strengthening Movement''' ({{Langx|kz|ߌߦߌߞߊ ߕߌߕߎ߲ ߊߜߓߊߙߊ ߊߙߊ|Ìyíká Títún Agbára Ara|Cycle of Renewing Body Strength}}), also known as the '''Sanyanian Reformation''' was a period of radical institutional, military, and cultural reform in the [[Sanyan|State of Sanyan]] initiated in the mid-17th century. Triggered by the [[Òrìṣáyọmí Mission]] of 1665 and the realization of the technological disparity between Sanyan and colonial powers, the movement sought to adopt Western technology and administrative practices while strictly preserving Sanyanian cultural and religious sovereignty.

The movement is widely regarded by historians as the pivotal era that allowed Sanyan to avoid later colonization during the 19th-century [[Conquest of Kaftia]]. It transitioned the state from a regional trading hub into a centralized, armed [[W:Merchant republic|merchant republic]] capable of enforcing the anti-slavery policies of the [[Kafto-Ethiopian Charter]] in 1793. The philosophy underpinning the movement—''Àjèjì'' ({{Literal translation|selective absorption of the foreign}})—remains a core tenet of modern Sanyanian political thought.

